// A constructor declared on a generic `given[R ...]` block must build the
// wrapper, not hand back its argument.
//
//     type mutable Boxed[R Mark](value R);
//     given[R Mark] Boxed[R] {
//         public new(r R) Boxed[R] = { return Boxed[R](r); };
//     };
//     let b = Boxed.new(Small(7));     -- R is inferred from the argument
//
// `samples/cat` does exactly this at its `BufReader.new(file)` call. The type
// checker got `R` right -- the generated symbol was `BufReader_Std_Os_File` --
// but codegen initialized the result temporary from the ARGUMENT instead of the
// constructed value:
//
//     struct BufReader_Std_Os_File_d117 _t594 = (_t593);   // _t593 is a Std_Os_File
//
// clang then rejected the C with `member reference base type 'intptr_t' is not
// a structure or union`, which is not a sentence about anything the user wrote.
// A missing member used to degrade into a field access the same way; this is
// the same shape of failure one level down -- sema letting something through
// and codegen emitting a type it never checked.
//
// The trigger is the receiver's type arguments being left to INFERENCE, not
// anything about managed types or about construction. `type` (value) fails the
// same way -- see `generic_receiver_param_not_method_param_regression`, which
// also covers a plain static method with no construction in it.
//
// These two spell out the same call and only one of them is broken:
//
//   * `Boxed.new(Small(7))`        -- receiver type args inferred, BROKEN
//   * `Boxed[Small].new(Small(9))` -- written out, always worked
//
// Every `.new(` in `tests/compiler-cases` before this was on a non-generic type
// (`Command`, `Date`, `ClockTime`), so a constructor that has to infer its
// receiver's type arguments from the argument list was untested.
//
// A single explicit `Boxed[Small].new(...)` ANYWHERE in the program masks the
// defect -- before or after the inferred call -- because that instantiation
// lays down a correct entry the inferred path then reuses. This case therefore
// contains no explicit call at all; adding one "to also cover the other form"
// would make it pass while the bug is still there.

trait Mark {
    mark(self) Int;
};

type Small(v Int);

given Small as Mark {
    public mark(self) Int = self.v;
};

type mutable Boxed[R Mark](value R);

given[R Mark] Boxed[R] {
    public new(r R) Boxed[R] = {
        return Boxed[R](r);
    };

    public get(self) R = self.value;
};

let main() Int = {
    let b = Boxed.new(Small(7));
    println(b.get().mark().to_string());
    return 0;
};
